From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:19:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/66] ipq40xx: add support for Arista W-118

Arista W-118 is a Wi-Fi 5 wall-plate access point with a third radio, a
1 GbE PoE uplink and three 1 GbE LAN ports.

Hardware specification:
- SoC: Qualcomm IPQ4019 (4x Cortex-A7)
- RAM: 512 MiB DDR3L
- Flash: 32 MiB SPI NOR (Macronix MX25L25635E), 256 MiB SLC NAND
  (Micron MT29F2G08ABAEA)
- Ethernet (the IPQ4019's switch, QCA8075 PHYs):
  - uplink: 1 GbE, PoE in (802.3af/at)
  - lan1-lan3: 1 GbE; lan3 can supply PoE (802.3af)
  - two RJ-45 jacks wired only to each other (pass-through)
- Wi-Fi:
  - IPQ4019: 2.4 GHz 2x2 802.11n and 5 GHz 2x2 802.11ac
  - QCA9882 (PCIe): 2.4 or 5 GHz 2x2 802.11ac, the stock firmware's
    scanning radio
- Bluetooth LE: NXP Kinetis controller (HCI over UART)
- LEDs: power, uplink, 2.4 GHz, 5 GHz, scanning radio, one for the
  three LAN ports (all green)
- Button: reset
- Power: PoE or 12 V DC
- UART: internal 4-pin header J12, 3.3 V TTL, 115200 8N1; pin 1 is
  3.3 V (leave it unconnected), pin 2 GND, pins 3 and 4 TX and RX

MAC addresses:
- uplink: 0:ART 0x0 (label)
- lan1-lan3: 0:ART 0x6 (label - 1)
- Wi-Fi: from the calibration data

Calibration data for the three radios is read from 0:ART through NVMEM
cells (0x1000 and 0x5000 for the IPQ4019, 0x9000 for the QCA9882).

The panel's LAN 1-3 are switch ports 2-4; port 1 is not wired. The
uplink and LAN 1-3 are all in the LAN by default, with DHCP. The
uplink's LED is green while it has a link, as on the Arista W-318.

Flash layout: the stock firmware keeps two slots, each a kernel in SPI
NOR (0:HLOS, 0:HLOS1) and a root in NAND (the first and second 64 MiB).
It does not use the upper 128 MiB of the NAND. OpenWrt takes that half
as UBI, with the kernel in a volume, and leaves both stock slots as
they are.

Flash instructions (console needed):
1. Stop U-Boot: any key stops it, but it shows no countdown, so send
   keys from power-on. Do not let the stock firmware boot meanwhile:
   its boot script saves the U-Boot environment on every boot.
2. Boot the initramfs from a TFTP server (nothing is saved):
     setenv ipaddr <AP address>; setenv serverip <TFTP server>
     setenv bootargs console=ttyMSM0,115200n8
     tftpboot 0x84000000 openwrt-ipq40xx-generic-arista_w-118-initramfs-zImage.itb
     bootm 0x84000000
3. Back up all MTD partitions; 0:ART holds the unit's calibration data
   and MAC addresses and exists nowhere else.
4. Set the boot command, in one environment write:
     fw_setenv -s - <<'EOF'
     owrt_args console=ttyMSM0,115200n8
     owrt_ubi setenv mtdids nand0=nand0; setenv mtdparts mtdparts=nand0:0x8000000@0x8000000(ubi)
     owrt_load ubi part ubi && ubi read 0x84000000 kernel && iminfo 0x84000000
     owrt_boot run owrt_ubi && run owrt_load && bootm 0x84000000
     bootcmd setenv bootargs ${owrt_args}; run owrt_boot; run ubootscript
     EOF
5. Copy the sysupgrade image to /tmp, write it and reboot:
     sysupgrade -n /tmp/openwrt-ipq40xx-generic-arista_w-118-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin
   It creates the UBI in the free half of the NAND.

If OpenWrt's kernel volume is missing or fails its check, the stock
boot script (ubootscript) runs as before. Later upgrades are ordinary
sysupgrades.

Return to stock: fw_setenv bootcmd 'run ubootscript', then reboot.

The kernel is a zImage in an uncompressed FIT: this U-Boot has no LZMA,
and the zImage also fits a stock 5 MiB kernel slot.

Known limitations:
- lan3's PoE output (GPIO 28) stays off.
- The LAN LED has no trigger: it stands for three ports, and the
  netdev trigger follows one.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
